## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit arithmetic, two's-complement negation, and branchless masking, all crucial for low-level optimizations in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit representation via hi/lo limbs
- Two's-complement negation across limb boundaries
- Branchless bit masking using bitwise operations
- Edge cases: x==0 and bit in hi vs lo limb

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 extractLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = (~x.lo) + 1;
  uint64_t hiNeg = (~x.hi) + (loNeg == 0);
  uint128 mask;
  mask.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  mask.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  return mask;
}
```

## Examples
- Input: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}` → Output: `{hi: 0, lo: 0b1000}`
- Input: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}` → Output: `{hi: 0b1000, lo: 0}`

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel parity computation with pure bitwise ops.\n\n## Key Concepts
- 128-bit lane handling via two 64-bit halves\n- In-place parity reduction using shifts\n- Bitwise masking to extract per-byte results\n\n## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 perByteParity128(uint128 x){
  uint64_t lo = x.lo, hi = x.hi;
  lo ^= lo >> 4; lo ^= lo >> 2; lo ^= lo >> 1;
  hi ^= hi >> 4; hi ^= hi >> 2; hi ^= hi >> 1;
  uint64_t mask = 0x0101010101010101ULL;
  return (uint128){ .lo = lo & mask, .hi = hi & mask };
}
```
