## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit value to a single parity bit using only bitwise ops, no branching.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based parity reduction
- Cross word reduction
- Constant-time bit twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (uint8_t)(x & 1);
}
```

